  <page>
    <title>James K. Vardaman</title>
    <id>231526</id>
    <revision>
      <id>24945299</id>
      <timestamp>2005-10-07T03:31:29Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Whobot</username>
        <id>393532</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>Recat per [[WP:CFD]] Category:U.S._Senators_from_Mississippi to Category:United_States_Senators_from_Mississippi</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''James Kimble Vardaman''' ([[July 26]], [[1861]] - [[June 25]], [[1930]]) was an [[United States|American]] politician from the [[U.S. state]] of  [[Mississippi]]. 

Vardaman was raised in [[Yalobusha County, Mississippi]], and eventually studied law and became editor of a newspaper, the ''[[Greenwood Commonwealth]]'' (still in publication today). As a [[United States Democratic Party|Democrat]], he served in the state house of representatives from [[1890]] to [[1896]] and was speaker of that body in [[1894]].  He was a major in the [[United States Army]] during the [[Spanish-American War]].  After two failed attempts in [[1895]] and [[1899]], Vardaman won the governorship in [[1903]] and served one 4-year term ([[1904]]-[[1908]]), advocating a policy of extreme racism against African-Americans, even to the point of supporting lynching in order to maintain Vardaman's vision of white supremacy.  He was elected to the [[United States Senate|U.S. Senate]] and served one term lasting from [[1913]] until [[1919]], having been defeated in his reelection bid in [[1918]]. The main factor in his defeat was his opposition to the [[Declarations_of_war_in_the_United_States|Declaration of War]] which had enabled the United States to enter [[World War I]].

{{start box}}
{{succession box|
 before=[[Andrew H. Longino]]|
 title=[[List of Governors of Mississippi|Governor]] of [[Mississippi]]|
 after=[[Edmond Noel]]|
 years=1904-1908
}}
{{end box}}

[[Category:1861 births|Vardaman, James K.]]
[[Category:1930 deaths|Vardaman, James K.]]
[[Category:Governors of Mississippi|Vardaman, James K.]]
[[Category:Spanish-American War people|Vardaman]]
[[Category:United States Senators from Mississippi|Vardaman, James K.]]</text>
