        <ip>216.162.156.20</ip>
      </contributor>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''William Bennett''' is a [[United Kingdom|British]] [[flute]] player, who has played with most of the major British [[orchestra]]s. He also has a career as a soloist. He developed an instrument, the '[[flauto di bassetto]]', which extends the range of the flute down a minor third, and one of his recordings, [[Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart|Mozart]]'s Concerto K 218, features this. In the late 1960's, his work on flute acoustics, in collaboration with other British flutists and the British flute maker Albert Cooper, helped vastly to improve the intonation of the modern flute. The resulting method of tuning is commonly called the Bennett-Cooper scale. He was awarded a lifetime achievement award by the [http://www.nfaonline.org National Flute Association] in 2002, and he was appointed an [[Order of the British Empire|OBE]] by the [[Elizabeth II of the United Kingdom|Queen]] in 1995.

[[Category:Flautists|Bennett, William]]
[[Category:Officers of the British Empire|Bennett, William]]

{{UK-musician-stub}}
{{woodwind-musician-stub}}</text>
    </revision>
