  <page>
    <title>Prince Marko</title>
    <id>231555</id>
    <revision>
      <id>41152638</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-25T10:53:08Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Mushroom</username>
        <id>34467</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>revert: I had incorrectly tagged the images as &quot;no source&quot;</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{| align=right
|[[Image:Marko - frescoe.jpg|left|frame|Contemporary frescoe of Marko, Marko's monastery, 14th century]]
|-
|[[Image:Marko - Mina.jpg|left|thumb|118px|Marko's portrait, by [[Mina Karadzic]]]]
|-
|[[Image:Marko - money.jpg|left|frame|Coin minted by Marko, Belgrade museum of applied arts]]
|}
'''Prince Marko''' (also rendered King Marko in English; [[Serbian language|Serbian]] ''Краљевић Марко (Kraljević Marko)'',[[Macedonian language|Macedonian]]: ''Крале Марко (Krale Marko)'', [[Bulgarian language|Bulgarian]] ''Крали Марко (Krali Marko)'') (around [[1335]] &amp;mdash; [[1395]]) was the son of King [[Vukashin|Vukašin]] (''Вукашин'') from the Serbian-originated ''Mrnjavčević'' (Serbian:''Мрњавчевић'') dynasty, who ruled in modern-day central [[Republic of Macedonia|Macedonia]], with his capital in [[Prilep]]. (Sometimes, the Macedonians sources quote the name &quot;Mrnjavčevci (Мрњавчевци)&quot; for the dynasty). The dynasty originated from modern-day Herzegovina. After his father died fighting against the Turks in [[1371]], Marko became a Turkish [[vassal]]. He died in the [[Battle of Rovine]].

Marko turned into a hero of many songs of [[Serbian epic poetry|Serbian]], Bulgarian and Macedonian epic poetry. Folklore remembers him as a respected protector and saviour of the local population during the Turkish yoke. It is unclear why he became such an important figure of epic poetry, contrary to his relatively small historical role.

==Marko in [[Serb]]ian epic==
Songs attribute to Marko superhuman strength: he can squeeze water from [[cornel]] dried for nine years; his favourite weapon is a [[mace]] of 66 [[oka (measure)|oka]] (85 [[kilogram]]s); he chose his horse because it was the only horse he could not throw over his shoulder. More importantly, they assert his knightly valour. Even while he could be short-tempered, he is a protector of the poor and helpless and a guardian of law and order, even when that is to his disadvantage. In one song he mourns because he killed a hero better than himself; in another, he says who by right should inherit the throne of the [[Serbian Empire]] even while his father could become the ruler if Marko lies. It is said that he decided to die when [[gun]]s were invented and when he saw that &quot;every coward can kill a hero&quot; easily, from a distance. After his death he sleeps in a cave, which is a typical [[king in the mountain]] motif.

Marko's [[horse]] is named ''Šarac (Шарац)'' (meaning &quot;Dappled&quot;). The horse is depicted as a match for the prince's strength, and also that drinks [[wine]] with him. Folk songs often begin with: &quot;He drinks wine, oh prince Marko;/half he drinks, half he gives to Šarac&quot;. Marko's chosen woman-protector is a [[Slavic fairies#Vila|vila]] called ''Ravijojla'' (Равијојла), whose name, according to the lingusist, comes from the Celtic origins and connects the myth of Prince Marko with the older Celtic myths in the Balkan area. 

His mother ''Jevrosima'' (''Јевросима'') is unknown to history, although Marko Kraljević has been recorded (along with his brother ''Andrеja'' (''Андреја'', sometimes known as ''Andrija'') in the historical chronicles of [[Vukashin|King Vukašin]]. In [[Serbian epic poetry]] Mother Jevrosima is a symbol of justice, moral, and Christian view of the world.

==Marko in [[Bulgarians|Bulgarian]] and [[Macedonians (ethnic group)|Macedonian]] epic==

When Krale (Krali) Marko was born, three [[narecnitsi]] (fate-fairies) appeared and fortold him he will become a hero and will replace his father - the king. King [[Vukashin|Vukašin]] heard this and threw his son on a basket in the river to get rid of him. But a [[samodiva]], called Vila, found him and brought him up as his foster mother. Because Marko suckled samodiva's milk he acquired supernatural powers and started fighting against the Turks. He had a winged horse, called Sharkolia (means Dappled) and a step sister - the samodiva Gyura.

When reading the Bulgarian legends of Krali Marko, one would find important fragments of pagan Slavic mythology and beliefs, although Bulgarian folk epos was created as late as 14-18th century.

==External links==
*[http://markokraljevic.uzice.net The book on Marko Kraljevic - Marko, The King's Son - Hero of The Serbs] ''by Clarence A. Manning, O. Muiriel Fuller, illustrated by Alexander Key''
* [http://www.sacred-texts.com/neu/hbs/ Heroic Ballads of Servia] ''translated by George Rapall Noyes and Leonard Bacon'', 1913
*[http://www.crsn.com/studiostrip/markokraljevic Web comic strip]

[[Category:Epics]]
[[Category:Slavic mythology]]
[[Category:Princes|Marko]]
[[Category:Serbian history]]
[[Category:History of Bulgaria]]

[[sr:Марко Краљевић]]
[[mk:Крале Марко]]</text>
    </revision>
