  <page>
    <title>Carmen Sandiego</title>
    <id>231571</id>
    <revision>
      <id>42147134</id>
      <timestamp>2006-03-04T03:15:03Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <ip>64.212.45.121</ip>
      </contributor>
      <comment>/* Carmen Sandiego in other media */</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">[[Image:Carmen_sandiego.jpg|right|300px||thumb|Carmen Sandiego, as she appeared in ''Where in the World...Treasures of Knowledge'']]
'''Carmen Isabela Sandiego''' is the eponymous [[fictional character]] featured in a long-running series of  [[educational game]]s and [[television show]]s in the [[United States]] and [[Canada]]. As a [[Crime|criminal]] mastermind and [[villain]], Sandiego was an integral part of the series, which originally focused on teaching [[geography]] and [[history]] (though the series later branched out into [[mathematics]] and [[English language|English]]). The character later appeared in a series of [[book]]s and on many licensed products.  She is typically depicted wearing a red [[fedora hat]] and [[trench coat]].

==Game series==
''Carmen Sandiego'' was originally conceived in [[1983]] by ex-Disney artist Gene Portwood, Dane Bingham, and Lauren Elliott at [[Brøderbund|Brøderbund Software]]. The concept for the product evolved from a game the Carlstons (founders of Broderbund) played as kids, using the world almanac to play quiz games. To inspire kids to learn geography, the creators wanted a female &quot;heroine&quot; whose name sounded exotic but was unmistakably simple to pronounce. The name &quot;Carmen Sandiego&quot; is somewhat of a play on [[Indiana Jones]]. The first software game, &quot;Where in the World is Carmen Sandiego?,&quot; was released in [[1985]]. This was followed by &quot;Where in the USA?&quot;, &quot;Where in Europe?&quot; and &quot;Where in the World...Jr. Detective Edition,&quot; all geography-based games. &quot;Where in Time&quot;/&quot;Great Chase through Time&quot; and &quot;Where in America's Past&quot; were the history-based games, &quot;Word Detective&quot; was the English-based game, &quot;Math Detective&quot; was the mathematics-based game, and &quot;Where in Space is Carmen Sandiego?&quot; was the space/astronomy-based game.

The first seven games were each awarded one or more [[SPA award|SPA Excellence in Software Awards]], particularly for their educational effort.

The series of games are now being released by [[The Learning Company]], which purchased [[Brøderbund]] in the [[1990s]]. The first title released by The Learning Company was &quot;Carmen Sandiego's Think Quick Challenge&quot; a quiz game.

In 2004, [[Bam! Entertainment]] released &quot;[[Carmen Sandiego: The Secret of the Stolen Drums]]&quot; to the GameCube, XBox, and Playstation2. It was the first 3D game for the franchise.

==Carmen Sandiego in other media==
Carmen's character has also appeared in three television shows, two of them [[Game show|game shows]] that aired on [[Public Broadcasting Service|PBS]] and later on Nickelodeon. The first was ''[[Where in the World is Carmen Sandiego?]],'' which aired from [[September]] [[1991]] to September [[1996]]. It was followed by ''[[Where in Time is Carmen Sandiego?]],'' which was on until September [[1998]]. The third show was an animated series titled ''[[Where on Earth is Carmen Sandiego?]],'' which debuted in [[February]] [[1994]] on [[Fox Broadcasting Company|Fox]] and ran for three seasons.

The ''World'' series, which was well known for a memorable theme song performed by [[Rockapella]] (who also gave clues to the contestants through song during the show itself), was hosted by [[Greg Lee]], with the chief of ACME Crime Lab played by [[Lynne Thigpen]].  The game provided a prize of a trip to anywhere around the continental USA for the winner, later expanded to a trip anywhere in North America; unfortunately, the requirements for winning the final round increased as the show went on, making it harder to win the trip. The first and third (final) round required knowledge of [[geography]]; however, the second round required less geographical knowledge, instead requiring contestants to play a game of [[Concentration (game)|Concentration]], fitting the geography-education theme by making the Concentration &quot;cards&quot; locations, people or ideas related to the object that had been &quot;stolen&quot; in that day's episode.

The ''Time'' series, hosted by [[Kevin Schinick]], refocused the show on history, but was otherwise similar to the previous show's format.  The final round of this show, however, involved answering various history related questions to open six &quot;time doors&quot;; if a question was answered correctly, the player walked through the door.  If it was answered incorrectly, however, the player had to turn a crank, pull a lever, or some other task that ate into their 90-second time limit.  If they got through all the doors, they received a [[personal computer]] as their prize.

In the ''Earth'' series, Carmen was voiced by [[Rita Moreno]], and the series won an [[Emmy]] in [[1995]]. Its episodes have subsequently been repeated on both the [[ABC Family|Fox Family Channel]] and the [[i (TV network)|Pax]] network.

In addition, Carmen was the &quot;spokesperson&quot; for [[Amtrak]] in spring [[1998]]. Also, she had a [[cameo appearance|cameo role]] on the show [[Animaniacs]] during the &quot;Acquaintances&quot; episode.

A Carmen Sandiego movie is apparently in the works, possibly slated for release sometime in 2006 or 2007. There has been no official cast or crew announced as of yet.

==Trivia==	 
* In the computer game, &quot;Where in the World is Carmen Sandiego? Treasures of Knowledge&quot;, it is revealed Carmen was a [[child prodigy]], having won a substantial amount of money at a game show when she was ten years old.
* In the Where on Earth? series, Carmen was a detective at ACME and built the &quot;Chief&quot; cyborg, as seen on the DVD of Where on Earth? subtitled, &quot;Into the Maelstrom&quot; and released by Sterling Entertainment, circa 2003.
*Carmen Sandiego, it is rumored, was named after a 'madame' in Hawaii.
* Rumors, spread through AOL chatrooms (circa 1994,) state that Carmen went &quot;[[Going commando|commando]]&quot; under her trenchcoat, though, in the deluxe version of the ''Where in the World?'' game, a white skirt is shown under the coat as she runs through Chinatown in San Francisco. In more recent games, she's known to wear a catsuit, showing off her figure.
* When &quot;Carmen Sandiego&quot; is typed into Google Images, an old &quot;spec&quot; test movie poster sometimes appears, with Catherine Zeta-Jones billed as starring in &quot;Where is Carmen Sandiego?&quot; motion picture. No further information exists about a movie with Ms. Zeta-Jones, and further urban myth has the movie poster originating from the Bonanza High School print-tech lab, circa 1996.
* Previously, if &quot;Where in the world is Carmen Sandiego&quot; was typed into [[Google]], &quot;[[Cairo]], [[Egypt]]&quot; would appear as an answer that is separate from any search results.[http://content.ytmnd.com/content/d/3/d38d65daad7c45b898f0adfc607de365.jpg] This is a reference to the original computer game, where Cairo was the final location in which the player captures her.

==See also==
*[[List of Carmen Sandiego products]]
*[[List of Carmen Sandiego characters]]

==External links==
