        <ip>200.117.190.2</ip>
      </contributor>
      <text xml:space="preserve">In [[music]], '''melisma''' is the technique of changing the [[musical note|note]] (pitch) of a syllable of text while it is being sung. Music sung in this style is referred to as ''melismatic'', as opposed to ''syllabic'', where each syllable of text is matched to a single note. Music of the ancient cultures used melismatic techniques to achieve a hypnotic trance in the listener, useful for early mystical initiation rites ([[Eleusinian Mysteries]]) and religious worship. This quality is still found in much Hindu and Muslim religious music today. In western music, the term most commonly refers to [[Gregorian Chant]], but may be used to describe music of any genre, including [[baroque]] singing and later [[gospel music|gospel]]. [[Aretha Franklin]] is generally considered to be one of the better modern employers of the technique.

Melisma first appeared in written form in some genres of Gregorian Chant, with the earliest written appearance around [[900]] AD. where it was used in certain sections of the [[Mass (music)|Mass]]. The [[gradual]] and the [[alleluia]], in particular, were characteristically melismatic, for example, while the tract is not, and repetitive melodic patterns were deliberately avoided in the style. The Byzantine rite also used melismatic elements in their music, which developed roughly concurrently to the Gregorian chant.

The hymn tune &quot;[[Gloria (hymn tune)|Gloria]]&quot; by [[Edward Shippen Barnes]], to which the hymn [[Angels We Have Heard On High]] is usually sung, contains one of the most melismatic sequences in popular Christian hymn music, on the &quot;o&quot; of the word &quot;Gloria&quot;.

Melisma is today commonly used in Middle Eastern popular music.  Melisma is also commonly featured in Western [[popular music]], which has been heavily influenced by [[African American music]]al and vocal techniques, by artists such as [[Whitney Houston]], [[Stevie Wonder]], [[Luther Vandross]], [[Mariah Carey]], [[Celine Dion]], [[Beyoncé Knowles]], and [[Christina Aguilera]].

Melisma is also the name of a computer system for performing various types of [[musical analysis]] (such as [[Metre (music)|metrical]], grouping, [[Harmony|harmonic]], [[Key (music)|key]], and [[Counterpoint|contrapuntal]] analysis) on [[MIDI]] representations of melodies (Temperley, 2001).

==References==
* Richard L. Crocker: &quot;Melisma&quot;. [[Grove Dictionary of Music and Musicians|Grove Music]] online, ed. L. Macy, accessed 27 Mar 2005.
* [http://www.internet-encyclopedia.org/wiki.phtml?title=Melisma Internet-Encyclopedia article &quot;Melisma&quot;]
* David Temperley, The Cognition of Basic Musical Structures. MIT Press, 2001.
==External links==
* [http://www.bartleby.com/61/69/M0206900.html American Heritage Dictionary of the English Language entry on &quot;melisma&quot;]
* [http://www.music.vt.edu/musicdictionary/textm/Melisma.html Virginia Tech Multimedia Music Dictionary entry on melisma]
* [http://www.link.cs.cmu.edu/music-analysis/ The Melisma Music Analyzer]

[[Category:Musical techniques]]
[[de:Melisma]]
[[eo:Melismo]]
[[es:Melisma]]
[[fi:Melisma]]
[[pl:Melizmat]]</text>
    </revision>
