      <text xml:space="preserve">The '''Index Catalogue (IC)''' &amp;mdash;also known as the '''Index Catalogue of Nebulae''', the '''Index Catalogue of Nebulae and Clusters of Stars''', '''IC I''', or '''IC II'''&amp;mdash; is a [[catalogue]] of [[galaxy|galaxies]], [[nebula]]e and [[star cluster]]s that serves as a [[supplement]] to the [[New General Catalogue]]. It was first published in [[1895]], and has been expanded to list more than 5,000 objects, known as the '''IC objects'''.

The catalogue was compiled by [[J. L. E. Dreyer]] in the [[1880s]], who published it as two [[appendix|appendices]] (IC I &amp;amp; IC II) to the New General Catalogue. It summarized the discoveries of galaxies, clusters and nebulae between [[1888]] and [[1905]].

==See also==

* [[List of IC objects]]
* [[General Catalogue]]
* [[Revised New General Catalogue]]
* [[Revised Index Catalogue]]
* [[:Category:NGC objects]]
* [[:Category:IC objects]]

==External links==

* [http://www.ngcic.org/scanned/iconline/ IC Catalogue by the NGC/IC Project] - official site.

[[de:Index-Katalog]]
[[it:Index Catalogue]]
[[ja:インデックスカタログ]]
[[zh:IC&amp;#22825;&amp;#20307;&amp;#21015;&amp;#34920;]]
[[Category:Astronomical catalogues]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Peter of Blois</title>
    <id>231849</id>
    <revision>
      <id>35980152</id>
      <timestamp>2006-01-20T17:59:37Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>TimBentley</username>
        <id>531594</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>better link</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Peter of Blois''' ([[1135]] (?) - [[1203]] (?)) was a [[France|French]] poet and diplomat who wrote in [[Latin]].

Peter of Blois studied [[law]] and [[theology]] at the [[University of Paris|Sorbonne]].  It was probably during his student years that he composed a number of Latin [[sequence (poetry)|sequence]]s after the manner of the [[Goliard]]s, some of which were preserved in the ''[[Carmina Burana]]'' collection.  

Peter became the tutor to King [[William II of Sicily]] in [[1167]].  In around [[1173]] he went to [[England]], where he served [[Henry II of England|Henry II]] and [[Thomas Becket]], the [[Archbishop of Canterbury]], as a Latin secretary.  He later served as Latin secretary to [[Eleanor of Aquitaine]], Henry's widow.  Many of his letters still survive.


===External links===
[http://www.newadvent.org/cathen/11765a.htm Catholic encyclopaedia]
 
[[Category:1135 births|Blois, Peter of]]
[[Category:1203 deaths|Blois, Peter of]]
[[Category:Latin literature]]
[[fr:Pierre de Blois]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Walter of Chatillon</title>
    <id>231852</id>
    <revision>
      <id>39349317</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-12T15:38:36Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Docu</username>
        <id>8029</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>+[[category:Year of death missing]]</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Walter of Châtillon''' was a [[twelfth century]] [[France|French]] writer and [[theology|theologian]] who wrote in the [[Latin|Latin language]].  

He was educated at the [[University of Paris|Sorbonne]] under [[Etienne de Beauvais]].  It was likely during his college years that he wrote a number of Latin [[poem]]s in the [[Goliard]]ic manner that found their way into the ''[[Carmina Burana]]'' collection. 

During his lifetime, however, he was more esteemed for a long Latin [[Epic poetry|epic]] on the life of [[Alexander the Great]], the ''Alexandreis, sive Gesta Alexandri Magni'', a long poem in [[hexameter]]s, full of [[anachronism]]s; he depicts the [[Crucifixion]] of [[Jesus]] as having already taken place during the days of Alexander the Great.  Most of this long poem has been forgotten by now; it is remembered chiefly for one line:

:''Incidit in Scyllam qui vult vitare Charybdim'' (He who wishes to avoid [[Charybdis]] runs into [[Scylla]].)

In addition to his poems, Walter wrote a dialogue attacking [[Judaism]] and a treatise on the [[Trinity]].  

Walter died of the [[bubonic plague]] early in the [[thirteenth century]]
[[fr:Gautier de Châtillon]]

[[Category:12th century births]] 
[[Category:French poets]]
[[Category:French theologians]]
[[Category:Latin literature]]
[[Category:Medieval literature]]


==External links==
*[http://www.newadvent.org/cathen/15543c.htm ''Catholic Encyclopedia: Walter of Chatillon'']

[[Category:Year of death missing]]</text>
    </revision>
