  <page>
    <title>Chinstrap Penguin</title>
    <id>231938</id>
    <revision>
      <id>42007954</id>
      <timestamp>2006-03-03T04:41:37Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Khoikhoi</username>
        <id>657950</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>+{{bird-stub}}</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{Taxobox
| color = pink
| name = Chinstrap Penguin
| image = penguinu.jpg
| image_width = 250px
| image_caption = Chinstrap penguin hunting for [[Antarctic krill|krill]]
| regnum = [[Animal]]ia
| phylum = [[Chordate|Chordata]]
| classis = [[bird|Aves]]
| ordo = [[penguins|Sphenisciformes]]
| familia = [[Spheniscidae]]
| genus = '''''[[Pygoscelis]]'''''
| species = '''''P. antarctica'''''
| binomial = ''Pygoscelis antarctica''
| binomial_authority = [[Johann Reinhold Forster|Forster]], [[1781]]
}}

The '''Chinstrap Penguin''' (''Pygoscelis antarctica'') is a species of [[penguin]] which is found in the [[South Sandwich Islands]], [[Antarctica]], the [[South Orkney Islands|South Orkneys]], [[South Shetland Islands|South Shetland]], [[South Georgia]], [[Bouvet Island]], [[Balleny]] and [[Peter I Island]]. Their name derives from the narrow black band under their heads which makes it appear as if they are wearing black helmets, making them one of the most easily identified types of penguin.

Other names for them are &quot;Ringed Penguins&quot;, &quot;Bearded Penguins&quot;, and &quot;Stonecracker Penguins&quot; due to their harsh call. They grow to 75&amp;nbsp;cm and there are approximately 7,500,000 breeding pairs. Their diet mostly consists of [[krill]] with a little fish as well. They build circular nests from stones, and lay around two eggs, which are incubated by both the male and the female for shifts of five to ten days. The chicks hatch after about 35 days, and have fluffy grey backs and white fronts. The chicks stay in the nest for 20&amp;ndash;30 days before they go to join a creche. At around 50&amp;ndash;60 days old, they moult, gaining their adult plumage and go to sea. 


{{bird-stub}}

[[Category:Penguins]]

[[de:Zügelpinguin]]
[[fr:Manchot à jugulaire]]
[[it:Pygoscelis antarctica]]
[[lt:Antarktinis pingvinas]]
[[nl:Stormbandpinguïn]]
[[ja:ヒゲペンギン]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Frederick Rolfe</title>
    <id>231939</id>
    <revision>
      <id>39787931</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-15T21:50:36Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Caponer</username>
        <id>422817</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <text xml:space="preserve">[[Image:Fr. Rolfe III.JPG|right|150px|Fr. Rolfe]]

'''Frederick William Serafino August Lewis Mary Rolfe''', better known as '''Baron Corvo''' ([[July 22]], [[1860]] - [[October 25]], [[1913]]), was an [[England|English]] writer, [[novel]]ist, artist and eccentric.

==Life and work==
The son of a piano manufacturer, Rolfe was a [[homosexual]], and a number of his books can be read as more or less veiled descriptions of homosexuality. Rolfe converted to [[Roman Catholicism]] in [[1886]] and was confirmed by [[Henry Edward Cardinal Manning|Cardinal Manning]]. Rolfe took on the ambition to become a [[Holy Orders|priest]], but failed in this attempt due to his inability to concentrate on priestly studies. Rebuffed in this ambition, he spent most of the rest of his life as a freelance writer, mainly in London but eventually in [[Venice]], where, out of money and out of luck, he died from a stroke. He also executed a number of paintings and designs, including cover designs for some of his books.

[[Image:Rolfetitobiondi1890roma.jpg|thumb|right|250px|'''Tito Biondi''' Photograph by Rolfe; Rome, ca. [[1890]]]]

'Baron Corvo' was not a real title - he claimed he had been granted its use as a courtesy by an Italian countess, but nothing backs up this story. It is in fact his best known [[pseudonym]]; he also called himself &quot;Frank English,&quot; &quot;Frederick Austin,&quot; &quot;A. Crab Maid,&quot; and several other pseudonyms. More often he abbreviated his own name to &quot;Fr. Rolfe&quot; (an ambiguous usage, suggesting he was the priest he had hoped to become). As &quot;Baron Corvo&quot; he was an occasional contributor to the ''[[Yellow Book]]'' published by [[John Lane (publisher)|John Lane]], notably with ''Stories Toto Told Me'', humorous retellings of Italian peasant legends about the saints, later collected in book form with that title and with a larger sequel, ''In His Own Image''. These made his early reputation such as it was, and this was enlarged by his ''Chronicles of the House of Borgia'', a serious if idiosyncratic historical study in refulgently [[Baroque]] prose. His extensive and obsessive erudition about the [[Renaissance]] period, of which the ''Chronicles'' is the most solid result, bore fruit in his two loosely-linked and intensely imagined historical novels of the Borgia period, ''Don Tarquinio'' and ''Don Renato''. 

[[Image:Don Tarquinio.JPG|left|Rolfe's design for 'Don Tarquinio']]

Rolfe's fiction steers well clear of any 'mainstream'.  If any of his works still find interested readers today, it is mostly on account of his [[prose]] style; erudite, ornate, and somewhat ''precieux'', it belongs on the same shelf with [[Symbolism (arts)|Symbolist]] [[prose poetry]].  His most autobiographical novel is ''[[Nicholas Crabbe]]'' and his best-known by far is ''[[Hadrian the Seventh]]'' ([[1904]]), a fantasy autobiography in which an obscure literary Englishman is elected [[pope]] and moves forward with an ambitious programme to set the world to rights. He engaged in a number of ill-starred collaborations, notably with [[Robert Hugh Benson|R. H. Benson]] (brother of [[Edward Frederic Benson|E. F. Benson]] and [[A. C. Benson]]) on a book about St. [[Thomas à Becket]] (Rolfe's contribution to this is minimal), and with [[Harry Pirie-Gordon]], which gave rise to two books almost entirely Rolfe's work, namely ''Hubert's Arthur'', a historical fantasy about [[Arthur I, Duke of Brittany]], and ''The Weird of the Wanderer'', envisaged as a sequel to ''The World's Desire'' by [[Andrew Lang]]. 

==Bibliography==
Rolfe's principal works include:

*''Stories Toto Told Me'' ([[1898]])
*''Chronicles of the House of Borgia'' ([[1901]])
*''Tarcissus the Boy Martyr of Rome'' ([[1901]])
*''Nicholas Crabbe'' ([[1903-4]], posthumously published [[1958]])
*''Don Tarquinio'' ([[1905]])
*''Don Renato'' ([[1907-8]], printed [[1909]] but not published, posthumously published [[1963]])
*''Hubert's Arthur'' ([[1909-11]], posthumously published [[1935]]) 
*''The Weird of the Wanderer'' ([[1912]])
*''The Desire and Pursuit of the Whole'' ([[1909]], published [[1934]])
*''In His Own Image'' (posthumously published [[1926]])

==Further reading==

A biography, ''The Quest for Corvo'', was written by [[A. J. A. Symons]].

See also:-

Sexual Heretics; Male Homosexuality in English literature from 1850-1900 - an anthology.  London, Routledge, Keegan and Paul, 1970. Brian Reade (Ed.)

Love In Earnest; some notes on the lives and writings of English [[Uranian]] poets from 1889 to 1930. Routlege, Keegan and Paul.  London, 1970.  [[Timothy D'Arch Smith]].

[[Category:1860 births|Rolfe, Frederick]]
[[Category:1913 deaths|Rolfe, Frederick]]
[[Category:British writers|Rolfe, Frederick]]
[[Category:Gay writers|Rolfe, Frederick]]
[[Category:British photographers|Rolfe, Frederick]]
[[Category:British painters|Rolfe, Frederick]]
[[Category:Pederastic lovers|Rolfe, Frederick]]

[[it:Frederick Rolfe]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Christ: The Bootleg</title>
    <id>231940</id>
    <revision>
